.TH BIND 1
.SH NAME
bind, mount, unmount \- change name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind
[
.B \-abc
]
.I new old
.PP
.B mount
[
.B \-abc
]
.I /srv/service old
.PP
.B unmount
[
.I new
]
.I old
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bind
and
.I mount
change the file name space of the calling process (see
.IR fork (2)).
.I Old
is a file or directory already reachable in the current name space,
where the change is made.
.PP
For
.IR bind ,
.I new
is another file or directory already reachable in the name space.
After a successful
.IR bind ,
the name
.I old
also reaches what
.I new
reaches;
.I new
still reaches it too, unless the bind hid it.
.PP
.I Mount
opens
.I /srv/service
(see
.IR srv (3))
and attaches to the connection it names, which is almost always a
posted service.
.I New
below means the root of that service's tree.
Either both
.I old
and
.I new
must be directories, or neither must be.
.PP
The options say how
.I old
is changed:
.TP 10
(none)
Replace
.I old
by
.IR new .
If they are directories, this is a union of one directory.
.TP
.B \-b
Both must be directories.
Add
.I new
to the front of the union directory
.I old
already is, or start one.
.TP
.B \-a
Both must be directories.
Add
.I new
to the back of the union.
.TP
.B \-c
Accepted with any of the above; see BUGS.
.PD
.PP
.I Unmount
undoes what
.I bind
or
.I mount
did.
With two arguments, it undoes the bind or mount made with the same two
names.
With one, everything bound or mounted onto
.I old
is undone.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add a second tree of files ahead of
.BR /bin ,
searched first:
.IP
.EX
bind \-b /n/esp/vectra/bin /bin
.EE
.PP
Bring up a served tree and take it down again:
.IP
.EX
mount /srv/memfs /mnt
ls /mnt
unmount /srv/memfs /mnt
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/bind/main.odin
.br
.B cmd/mount/main.odin
.br
.B cmd/unmount/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (1),
.IR ns (1),
.IR bind (2),
.IR srv (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty on success.
It is
.BR usage
for a wrong argument count, and
.BR "bind failed" ,
.B "mount failed"
or
.B "unmount failed"
for an error the call answered, with the reason on standard error.
.SH BUGS
.B \-c
on
.I bind
and
.I mount
is accepted but reaches nothing: the underlying calls carry no creation
flag, so a union directory of more than one member has no member marked
to receive a create, and a create in it fails with
.B EPERM
no matter which member was bound with
.BR \-c .
A directory with exactly one member, the ordinary case, is unaffected.
.I Mount
takes no
.I spec
argument, so a service that serves more than one tree cannot be asked
for the second.
There is no
.BR \-q ,
so a failed bind or mount is never silent.
